  "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/bce3befee4d7ae90df59b7bfb17e965ca29615220ca6845a78cfaad3e02a0730.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b6cac24748d4a3990038ef7140943bd0947207456132e66ca6b7fb887be44164.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+1)^{2}$ | $(y-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-2,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(0,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(0,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/174965ef064b55b29326c4681f709a6dfcbc690673e2e5a7498e9a65c4a6af2c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
